  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn active period of showers and thunderstorms continues in a couple \nof phases as Straits low pressure pulls a cold front through Lower \nMi today. Clusters of showers and thunderstorms that are ongoing \nleading up to sunrise are within the low to mid level moisture axis \nahead of the cold front. This is where model soundings indicate a \ndeep layer of elevated instability, but with minimal CAPE density \nshown in both the soundings and implied by plan view depictions of \n850 mb LI around -2 C and sub 7 C/km 700-500 mb lapse rate. This \nelevated convection merges with scattered hybrid surface based \nactivity of similar intensity during the morning within and between \na prefrontal surface trough and the inbound cold front. \n\nThe early day activity adds some doubt about verification of model \npeak instability as the cold front sweeps through SE Mi this \nafternoon. However, HREF and REFS members all handle the ongoing \nactivity reasonably well and still suggest a respectable recovery in \ntime for the front to support a storm flare-up while overhead and \nbefore it exits into Ontario toward early evening. There is \notherwise no big change in the CAPE/shear environment as HREF mean \nsurface based CAPE holds below 1500 J/kg with bulk shear around 25 \nknots, good enough for multicell clusters capable of peak gusts \napproaching severe intensity as the primary hazard in the strongest \nstorms. The Marginal Risk remains in place to highlight this \npotential in SE Mi with a Slight Risk now just east of the border \nuntil the front exits early this evening. \n\nScattered showers trail the front, mainly during the evening, as the \n500 mb trough digs toward the eastern Great Lakes, but also as \nmoderate cold advection gains traction overnight. Increasing NW flow \noccurs across Lower Mi around the west flank of surface low pressure \ndeepening on the Ontario/Quebec border as the 500 mb trough deepens \nand closes off early Sunday. Model agreement maintains the trend on \nsolutions offering a larger mid level circulation closing off closer \nto Lower Mi during the day. There is also a well-defined reinforcing \nvorticity maximum holding consistency in model solutions as it digs \nin from the NW and moves overhead during the afternoon. Guidance \nprobabilities for rain appear underdone in this environment as \nscattered to numerous coverage is favored, especially Sunday \nafternoon and evening with a boost from daytime instability. \n\nThe low level thermal trough is firmly in place over the western \nGreat Lakes by Monday morning with low temperatures in the upper 40s \nstill within reach outside of the larger metro areas. Deterministic \nand ensemble model guidance continue to project single digit 850 mb \ntemperatures to start the day which rebound quickly as the mid/upper \nlevel low shifts eastward. A stray shower also remains in play, \nespecially toward the Thumb region, during the morning until \nconfluent NW flow develops to help ensure dry weather for the \nafternoon and Monday night. Slightly below normal highs in the upper \n70s are reachable as the thermal trough shifts eastward. \n\nWeak surface high pressure is supported by a mid/upper level short \nwave ridge Monday night and Tuesday. Dry weather and a few degrees \nof warming occur through mid week within the slowly progressive \nlarge scale flow. High temperatures return to the lower 80s during \nmid week while the next chance of rain is carried in by the current \nPac NW system. Extended range global models project it arrives in \nthe Great Lakes by Wednesday.",
